\v 12 I implore you to become¦127032 like me, brothers¦127039 and¦127039 sisters¦127039, because¦127035 I'm¦127034 also¦127036 just like you. You¦127041 haven't wronged¦127044 me in any way,
\v 13 but¦127046 you know¦127045 that it was a result of¦127048 my fleshly¦127051 weakness¦127049 that I originally preached¦127052 the¦127050 good¦127052 message¦127052 to¦127053 you.\f + \fr 4:13 \ft Scholars are unclear whether this might mean something like ill health, poor eyesight (see v15), or even a moral weakness, so we've left it as ambiguous in the translation as it is in the original.\f*
\v 14 Yet you didn't¦127066 mock or despise me, \add brothers and sisters,\add* for this trial¦127058 of¦127059 my¦127065 flesh¦127064, but¦127056 rather you received me as¦127071 God's messenger¦127072 like you might have received the \nd messiah¦127077 Yeshua¦127078\nd* himself.
